findings.problem:
For routing, the plug is simulated 0.65 m ahead of Pip (game.ts:267). The drawn end is Pip's right glove (handPosition, game.ts:289-293). cablePath only lifts the last 1.8 m off the floor (game.ts:10-20). The cable trails behind Pip because he is walking away from the reel, so its last segment passes through his boots, shins and belly to reach the hand in front.

Seen in:
- the Playground win push-in (cable-18: the gold cable goes between the boots into the socket; R2 bug 3 / P3, never fixed);
- the pillar wrap (cable-41);
- the crate drag (cable-51);
- Lunch thick and thin drags (cable-84, cable-130).

It's on screen in nearly every drag frame and in the reward shot, so it cheapens the 'hero cable' (DR-73, ART R15.3).

notes.models:
The hose model is good: a thick glossy tube with outline, a centripetal Catmull-Rom curve, a gentle sag when slack, and a rise to the glove over the last 1.8 m. It hugs pillar corners convincingly at 0.12 m clearance (cable-42). The thick Lunch cable's ink bands make it instantly distinct from thin leads (cable-82).

The visual bugs are interpenetration:
- through Pip's legs on every drag and in the win shot (P3 still open);
- through any box or shelf the plug is pressed into (the plug-inside-AABB exclusion);
- through light props and the boardroom glass corner;
- in Lunch, through everything that isn't a wall, including its own dolly.

No floor z-fighting was seen: the tube sits at y 0.09 with radius 0.1. The cable doesn't float, except the short arc from reel to hand when less than about 1.8 m is paid out (cable-74), which reads as 'held up'.
